@@s001 Algebraic types (ADT)
@@s002 <p><code>List(1, 2, 3)</code> calls <code>apply</code>. No cell exists yet: <code>apply</code> must reach the end before building.</p>
@@s003 <p><code>apply</code> separates the first element and calls itself on the rest. Each call leaves a <code>Cons</code> pending.</p>
@@s004 <p>With zero elements <code>apply</code> returns <b>Nil</b>, the empty list. It is the first thing really built.</p>
@@s005 <p><code>Cons(3, Nil)</code>: a cell holding 3 that points to Nil.</p>
@@s006 <p><code>Cons(2, …)</code> puts a cell <b>in front</b> of the one that already exists.</p>
@@s007 <p><code>Cons(1, …)</code>: the list is complete. It is built from the right, because each cell needs the rest in order to exist.</p>
@@s008 <p><b>head</b> is the first element: <b>1</b>.</p><p><b>tail</b> is <b>all the rest</b>: <code>List(2, 3)</code>, not the last element.</p>
@@s009 <p><code>val a = List(2, 3)</code>.</p>
@@s010 <p><code>val b = Cons(1, a)</code>: just one new cell. The rest of <code>b</code> <b>is</b> <code>a</code>, not a copy.</p>
@@s011 <p><code>val c = Cons(9, a)</code>: another cell, same rest.</p>
@@s012 <p>The cells 2 and 3 are shared by <code>a</code>, <code>b</code> and <code>c</code>. That is why <code>tail(b) eq a</code> is <b>true</b>: it is the same object in memory (checked).</p><p>Sharing is safe because lists are immutable.</p>
@@s013 dato:Cons (head + tail),spento:Nil,ora:shape you are looking at
@@s014 ora:sum (one shape or the other),dato:product (all the arguments together)
@@s015 ora:current expression,ok:result
@@s016 dato:types,ok:Nil works here,ora:Nothing (under all)
@@s017 Algebraic types (ADT)
@@s018 An algebraic type lists <b>all the shapes</b> that a value can have. A list is: empty, or an element followed by another list. Nothing else. From this idea come pattern matching and all the functions on lists.
@@s019 Two shapes, and that is all
@@s020 The course list (02-adt/Exercises.scala)
@@s021 <code>enum</code> declares a type with a <b>fixed</b> number of possible shapes. Each shape is a <code>case</code>.
@@s022 <code>A</code> is the type of the elements: <code>List[Int]</code>, <code>List[String]</code>… The <code>+</code> (covariance) is explained by the <a href="s03-varianza.html">page on variance</a>.
@@s023 First shape: <code>Nil</code>, the empty list. It contains no data.
@@s024 Second shape: <code>Cons</code>, a cell. It contains an element, <code>head</code>…
@@s025 …and the rest of the list, <code>tail</code>, which is <b>in turn a List</b>. The type is defined using itself: it is recursive.
@@s026 If you come from Java
@@s027 It is like a <code>sealed</code> interface with two implementations: an empty record <code>Nil</code> and a record <code>Cons(A head, List&lt;A&gt; tail)</code>. The compiler knows that no other shapes exist.
@@s028 Nil and Cons in a real value
@@s029 Building <code>List(1, 2, 3)</code>
@@s030 From the call to the cells
@@s031 Frequent doubt
@@s032 <b>In the list 1, 2, 3, 4 is the tail 4?</b> No. <code>head</code> is <b>1</b>, <code>tail</code> is <b>2, 3, 4</b>: everything except the first. The last element has no special name and to reach it you must go through the whole list.
@@s033 Why "algebraic": sums and products
@@s034 Definition (slide 3)
@@s035 A type generated by one or more <b>constructors</b>, each with zero or more arguments. The shapes of the various constructors are <b>summed</b> (a value is one <i>or</i> the other); each constructor is a <b>product</b> of its arguments (it contains them all <i>together</i>).
@@s036 Type
@@s037 Sum: the shapes
@@s038 Product: what each shape contains
@@s039 <code>List[A]</code>
@@s040 <code>Nil</code> or <code>Cons</code>
@@s041 <code>Cons</code> = an <code>A</code> and a <code>List[A]</code>
@@s042 <code>Tree[A]</code> (week 3)
@@s043 <code>Leaf</code> or <code>Branch</code>
@@s044 <code>Leaf</code> = an <code>A</code>; <code>Branch</code> = two <code>Tree[A]</code>
@@s045 <code>Option[A]</code> (week 3)
@@s046 <code>Some</code> or <code>None</code>
@@s047 <code>Some</code> = an <code>A</code>; <code>None</code> = nothing
@@s048 Practical consequence: if a type has two shapes, <b>every function on that type answers the question "which of the two do I have in hand?"</b>. That is why the functions on lists almost always have two cases: one for <code>Nil</code>, one for <code>Cons</code>.
@@s049 Sums of shapes, products of arguments
@@s050 The companion object: the functions of the list
@@s051 Next to the <code>enum</code> there is an <code>object</code> with the <b>same name</b>. It is called <i>companion object</i> and contains the functions on the list (like the <code>static</code> methods of Java).
@@s052 sum and apply (slide 4)
@@s053 Same name as the <code>enum</code>: it is its companion. The functions are called <code>List.sum(…)</code>, or just <code>sum(…)</code> after <code>import List.*</code>.
@@s054 <code>sum</code> looks at the shape of the list (pattern matching, <a href="s02-pattern-matching.html">next page</a>).
@@s055 The sum of the empty list is 0.
@@s056 Otherwise: the first element plus the sum of the rest.
@@s057 <code>apply</code> has a special name: <code>List(1, 2, 3)</code> is a shortcut for <code>List.apply(1, 2, 3)</code>.
@@s058 <code>A*</code> means "how many arguments you want of type A" (varargs, like <code>A...</code> in Java). Inside, <code>as</code> is a sequence.
@@s059 Zero arguments: the empty list.
@@s060 Otherwise: a cell with the first argument, and as the rest the list of the remaining arguments. <code>as.tail*</code> <b>unpacks</b> the sequence again into separate arguments, because <code>apply</code> wants them that way.
@@s061 List(1, 2, 3) with apply, then sum
@@s062 A shared tail
@@s063 Adding at the head copies nothing: the new cell points to the list that is already there.
@@s064 Three lists, two cells in common
@@s065 A single empty list for all types
@@s066 In the version of slide 3 with classes, <code>Nil</code> is declared like this:
@@s067 <code>sealed</code>: no one can add shapes outside this file. So the compiler knows which cases exist.
@@s068 <code>Nothing</code> is the type "at the bottom of all": it is a subtype of every type. Thanks to the <code>+</code>, <code>List[Nothing]</code> is a subtype of <code>List[Int]</code>, <code>List[String]</code>… So a single <code>Nil</code> works for all lists.
@@s069 In the course the version with <code>enum</code> is used, which is shorter. The two versions are equivalent.
@@s070 Nothing and covariance: one Nil for all types
